+  <section class="journal" id="roasters-journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <div class="journal-header">
+      <div>
+        <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <p class="journal-meta">From Origin to Cup: How We Source Our Beans</p>
+      </div>
+      <button type="button" class="summarize-btn" id="summarize-btn">&#10024; Summarize</button>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="summary-box" id="summary-box" hidden aria-live="polite">
+      <h4>The Short Version</h4>
+      <p class="summary-status" id="summary-status"></p>
+      <ul id="summary-list"></ul>
+    </div>
+
+    <article class="journal-body" id="journal-body">
+      <p>Every bag of coffee that leaves our roastery begins with a relationship, not a transaction. Long before a single bean hits our drum roaster, our sourcing team has spent months walking farms, cupping samples, and sharing meals with the growers who make our work possible. We believe great coffee is impossible without great people behind it, and that conviction shapes every decision we make about where our beans come from.</p>
+
+      <h3>Finding the Right Partners</h3>
+      <p>Each year, our head roaster and green coffee buyer travel to origin during harvest season. We visit smallholder farms and cooperatives in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, Peru, and Sumatra, focusing on producers who share our commitment to quality and sustainability. Rather than chasing the latest trendy micro-lot, we look for partners we can grow alongside for years. Today, more than seventy percent of our green coffee comes from farms we have worked with for at least five consecutive harvests.</p>
+      <p>On these trips we cup hundreds of samples, often at long tables set up right next to the drying beds. We score each coffee for sweetness, acidity, body, and clarity, but we also pay attention to things a score sheet can't capture: how a farm manages its water, how it treats its pickers, and whether the next generation is excited to keep the family business going.</p>
+
+      <h3>Paying Fairly and Transparently</h3>
+      <p>We buy directly whenever possible and pay well above Fairtrade minimums &mdash; on average, forty to sixty percent above the commodity market price for the same grade. We publish the price we paid per pound for every single-origin coffee on our website, along with the farm or cooperative name and region. Transparency keeps us honest, and it helps our customers understand that a fair price at the farm gate is what makes a truly exceptional cup possible.</p>
+      <p>We also offer pre-harvest financing to several of our long-term partners. Access to credit at fair terms lets farmers invest in new drying beds, better fermentation tanks, and replanting programs without turning to predatory lenders. In return, they reserve their best lots for us, and we both benefit from the stability.</p>
+
+      <h3>Caring for the Land</h3>
+      <p>Climate change is the single greatest threat to specialty coffee. Rising temperatures and unpredictable rainfall are pushing suitable growing land higher up the mountains every year. We prioritize farms that practice shade-growing, composting, and water conservation, and we contribute a portion of every bag sold to a reforestation fund that has planted more than 40,000 native trees across our partner regions since 2019.</p>
+
+      <h3>From Green to Roasted</h3>
+      <p>When green coffee arrives at our roastery, it's tested again for moisture, density, and defects before being stored in a climate-controlled room. We roast in small batches of no more than fifteen kilograms, developing a unique profile for each origin that highlights its natural character &mdash; the bright citrus of a washed Ethiopian, the chocolate depth of a Colombian Huila, or the earthy spice of a wet-hulled Sumatran. Every batch is cupped the following morning, and anything that doesn't meet our standard never makes it to the shelf.</p>
+      <p>Finally, we roast to order and ship within forty-eight hours, so the coffee in your cup is as fresh as the day it left the drum. It's a long journey from a hillside farm to your morning mug, and we're proud of every step along the way.</p>
+    </article>
+  </section>
